<http://data.silknow.org/object/48b5fb59-c111-30bf-87ea-ba341a4280de>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "This ewer is one of the largest and most impressive examples of a low-fire white pottery made in France in the middle years of the sixteenth century. Known as Saint-Porchaire ware, this group of elaborate and often architectural pieces is distinguished by the complex interlace designs of colored clays inlaid into the cream-colored earthenware body. These wares were believed to have been produced in the town of Saint-Porchaire in western France, but a Paris origin has also been suggested due to their technical sophistication and the ambition of their designs.[Jeffrey H. Munger, 2011]"@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/eba3d8ad-7a3c-39d9-9dd7-d237a5f90edb> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/ad6c1eb8-edc8-3490-9f68-ca907db83fc8> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/d026a18a-3e3f-3c4d-9890-ec9a8041cd7e> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/a1ab832a-997f-3540-9aa8-e0d1e9278d75> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/b010b949-af2f-3c1d-bdd0-26d6dbbed804> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "This ewer is one of the largest and most impressive examples of a low-fire white pottery made in France in the middle years of the sixteenth century. Known as Saint-Porchaire ware, this group of elaborate and often architectural pieces is distinguished by the complex interlace designs of colored clays inlaid into the cream-colored earthenware body. These wares were believed to have been produced in the town of Saint-Porchaire in western France, but a Paris origin has also been suggested due to their technical sophistication and the ambition of their designs.[Jeffrey H. Munger, 2011]"@en ;
        dc:identifier                  "17.190.1740" ;
        <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs>
                <https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/194243> .

